1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,642
Quarter past nine.

2
00:00:01,692 --> 00:00:02,492
Ines.

3
00:00:05,803 --> 00:00:06,603
The problem:

4
00:00:06,502 --> 00:00:10,764
a developer only learns a change broke
the standards after it has shipped,

5
00:00:10,814 --> 00:00:15,485
and a rule that is wrong for the environment
creates noise until someone owns it.

6
00:00:15,535 --> 00:00:20,379
This part shows Ines being told on every
push, and one wrong rule going back to Rob.

7
00:00:21,557 --> 00:00:22,628
Quarter past nine.

8
00:00:22,678 --> 00:00:24,559
Watch the handoff in this part.

9
00:00:24,609 --> 00:00:29,355
Ines fixes her API, and the cluster tells
her how she is doing on every push.

10
00:00:29,405 --> 00:00:33,218
Then one rule turns out to be
wrong for Kestrel's environment,

11
00:00:33,268 --> 00:00:37,391
and Rob changes the rule rather
than asking Ines to work around it.

12
00:00:38,564 --> 00:00:40,917
Two of the errors are gateway settings.

13
00:00:40,967 --> 00:00:42,827
In the Tyk section of her file,

14
00:00:42,877 --> 00:00:47,757
Ines turns on token authentication and
adds a rate limit of a hundred requests a

15
00:00:47,807 --> 00:00:49,174
minute on the upstream.

16
00:00:49,224 --> 00:00:50,714
Nothing else changes yet.

17
00:00:51,879 --> 00:00:53,225
She pushes the change.

18
00:00:53,275 --> 00:00:57,667
Normally her pipeline applies it;
here a small script plays that part.

19
00:00:57,717 --> 00:01:01,030
Each version of the spec
goes into its own ConfigMap,

20
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:04,013
and the API resource is
pointed at the new one.

21
00:01:04,063 --> 00:01:06,551
That update is what the Operator checks.

22
00:01:07,697 --> 00:01:09,199
Look at the warning line.

23
00:01:09,249 --> 00:01:13,295
The Tyk Operator evaluated the
change before Kubernetes stored it,

24
00:01:13,345 --> 00:01:17,639
and printed the result straight into
her terminal: twenty errors left.

25
00:01:17,689 --> 00:01:22,232
The change still went through, because
Rob's ruleset says warn, not block.

26
00:01:26,878 --> 00:01:27,918
In the Dashboard,

27
00:01:27,968 --> 00:01:32,278
Rob's platform standards have dropped
from five violations to three.

28
00:01:32,328 --> 00:01:34,715
Only the missing operation IDs remain.

29
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:40,177
The rest of the fixes live in
the OpenAPI document itself.

30
00:01:40,227 --> 00:01:42,803
Every operation gets an operation ID,

31
00:01:43,153 --> 00:01:46,882
payment IDs become random UUIDs
instead of guessable numbers,

32
00:01:46,932 --> 00:01:51,033
and every string and error response
gets proper limits and schemas.

33
00:01:52,199 --> 00:01:53,317
She pushes again.

34
00:01:53,367 --> 00:01:57,235
No warning this time, and the
preflight status is passed.

35
00:02:01,811 --> 00:02:04,041
Rob's standards now score a hundred.

36
00:02:04,091 --> 00:02:07,081
But the OWASP baseline
still reports two errors.

37
00:02:11,621 --> 00:02:15,271
Both come from the same rule:
server URLs must use HTTPS.

38
00:02:15,321 --> 00:02:18,711
They point at addresses the
Dashboard adds by itself,

39
00:02:18,761 --> 00:02:22,021
the gateway's internal
address and a relative path.

40
00:02:22,071 --> 00:02:24,228
Neither of them is in Ines's file.

41
00:02:25,619 --> 00:02:28,050
At Kestrel, TLS ends at the network edge,

42
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,923
so this rule is right in general
and wrong for this environment.

43
00:02:31,973 --> 00:02:33,436
Ines sends Rob a message.

44
00:02:33,486 --> 00:02:34,646
This is the handoff:

45
00:02:34,696 --> 00:02:39,609
the problem moves from the person following
the rules to the person who owns them.

46
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:42,463
Twenty past eleven.

47
00:02:42,513 --> 00:02:43,313
Rob.

48
00:02:46,392 --> 00:02:48,075
Rob's hands on the keyboard.

49
00:02:48,125 --> 00:02:51,480
Two lines override that one
rule from error to warning.

50
00:02:51,530 --> 00:02:53,708
Every other OWASP rule is untouched.

51
00:02:53,758 --> 00:02:57,051
He keeps it as a warning
rather than switching it off,

52
00:02:57,101 --> 00:03:00,950
so it still shows if a team ever
publishes a real HTTP address.

53
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:02,966
He applies it.

54
00:03:03,016 --> 00:03:06,096
The Operator updates the
ruleset in the Dashboard,

55
00:03:06,146 --> 00:03:11,354
the Dashboard re-checks every payments API,
and the Operator refreshes their status.

56
00:03:12,494 --> 00:03:16,331
The payments API is now
compliant against both rulesets.

57
00:03:20,966 --> 00:03:25,290
Ines refreshes her Governance
tab: compliant, zero errors.

58
00:03:29,295 --> 00:03:34,786
Across the estate, one API is compliant and
the two internal ones are Rob's backlog.

59
00:03:34,836 --> 00:03:36,370
It is Tuesday lunchtime.

60
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:41,845
Ines's API cost Rob one message and a
two-line commit, and nobody booked a meeting.
